-- postpartum week 79: Eating well while you're on a course of antibiotics (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   79,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well while you''re on a course of antibiotics',
   '"The antibiotic does its work quietly in the background — food''s job this week is just to make the days a little easier, alongside taking it exactly as prescribed."',
   'Antibiotics are common in the postpartum stretch — for mastitis, a urinary tract infection, a dental infection or similar. Take the antibiotic exactly as prescribed, including the full duration, unless your prescriber changes the plan — stopping early or skipping doses because you feel better is one of the more common reasons a course doesn''t fully work. Seek advice about any concerning reaction rather than continuing regardless. Food instructions differ by medicine and formulation — some antibiotics are best taken with a meal, some on an empty stomach, and some need to be spaced apart from dairy, iron, calcium or antacids. Check your pharmacy label now, since the menus below include dairy at most meals, and ask your pharmacist if anything is unclear. Nausea, a reduced appetite, mild discomfort or loose stools are common side effects and usually settle once the course ends. There''s no need to restrict your own diet during the course, and plain curd with live cultures can stay part of your meals if you tolerate it well; a gentler, more tolerable option is offered on the days it might help. This week walks through eating comfortably through the course, checking your specific medicine''s food instructions, and the signs that mean something more than an ordinary side effect is going on.',
   'If you''ve reacted to an antibiotic before: tell the prescriber before this course starts, even if the earlier reaction seemed mild — they may choose a different medicine or want to watch more closely. If you manage diabetes: some antibiotics and the illness being treated can both affect your glucose pattern — follow your own diabetes plan''s guidance, and contact your care team if you''re struggling to eat or drink normally. If you have an ongoing health condition or a weakened immune system: ask your doctor before adding a separate probiotic supplement — food sources like plain curd are generally fine, the extra caution is specifically for more complex health needs. Contact your prescriber promptly for a new rash or hives. Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, collapse, or a severe blistering/peeling rash needs emergency help right away (NHS guidance on antibiotic side effects). Frequent watery diarrhoea, blood in your stool, fever, or diarrhoea continuing after the course ends are also worth a prompt call to your prescriber.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or your appetite is smaller than usual, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Antibiotics are common in the postpartum stretch — for mastitis, a urinary tract infection, a dental infection or similar. Take it exactly as prescribed, including the full duration, unless your prescriber changes the plan.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If your stomach reacts to it — tolerable alternatives", "notes": "Nausea, a reduced appetite, mild abdominal discomfort or loose stools are common while on an antibiotic — the likelihood and severity depend on the specific medicine. The A option below leans gentler and more tolerable for a harder day; the B option is there for whenever your appetite is closer to normal. Smaller, more frequent meals of whatever you can manage are a reasonable adjustment; there''s no need to force a full plate. Worth knowing: Most reactions are mild and settle once the course ends. Contact your prescriber if a side effect is persistent, worsening, or you develop frequent watery diarrhoea, blood in your stool, fever, or signs of dehydration.", "breakfast_a": "Plain toast (2 slices) with a ripe banana and a small bowl of curd (150g) — a gentler option for a harder morning", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd — settling if your appetite is smaller today", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few soaked raisins", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin — often easier to keep down than a full glass of milk", "dinner_a": "Plain rice (1 cup cooked) with a thin moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers or dry toast"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Curd, live cultures, and what they actually do", "notes": "Plain, unsweetened curd or yogurt with live/active cultures can stay part of your regular diet through the course if you tolerate it well. Some specific probiotic strains have shown a possible benefit for antibiotic-related loose stools, but effects vary by strain and dose — ordinary curd is food, not a guaranteed prevention or cure. Ask your doctor before starting a separate probiotic supplement.", "breakfast_a": "Curd rice (1 cup, made with 150g curd) with a few pomegranate seeds", "breakfast_b": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Check the label, not just the name", "notes": "Antibiotic instructions differ by medicine and formulation — some can be taken with or without food, some are best taken with a meal, and some need separation from dairy, iron, calcium or antacids. Follow the pharmacy label, and ask your pharmacist or prescriber if it''s unclear, rather than guessing or copying a friend''s experience with a different antibiotic. Worth knowing: If you''re prescribed an iron or calcium supplement alongside your antibiotic, ask specifically whether they need to be spaced apart — this varies by antibiotic class.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Alcohol and antibiotics don''t always mix", "notes": "Certain antibiotics interact with alcohol and can cause an unpleasant reaction — flushing, nausea, a fast heartbeat. Ask your pharmacist whether yours is one of them before drinking at all, on top of the usual pregnancy/breastfeeding alcohol guidance below.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s more than an ordinary side effect", "notes": "Most side effects settle on their own once the course ends. A few things are different: contact your prescriber promptly for a new rash or hives. Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, collapse, or a severe blistering/peeling rash needs emergency help right away (NHS guidance on antibiotic side effects) — these can be signs of a more serious reaction, not an ordinary side effect. Worth knowing: If you''ve reacted to an antibiotic before, tell the prescriber before this course starts, even if the earlier reaction seemed mild.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Have you been taking the antibiotic exactly as prescribed this week — following the label''s food and timing instructions — even on the days you feel completely back to normal?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat taking your own medicine exactly as prescribed, including any food-timing instructions, with the same care you''d want for anyone else in your care?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own treatment and recovery deserve real follow-through, not just easing off once you feel better.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 80: Eating well as you adjust to having two children now (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   80,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well as you adjust to having two children now',
   '"Needing more help with your own meals right now isn''t a step back from how you managed before — it''s exactly what this stretch calls for."',
   'Use this week''s guidance if you are caring for more than one child. If you have recently given birth again, your current recovery stage and care plan take priority over the week count from your earlier birth — come back to this content whenever it actually fits your situation. Caring for more than one child at once stretches your own eating thinner than a single-child stretch did — less time, less help sometimes, more happening at once. Leaning on low-preparation, batch-friendly meals this week isn''t a lesser way to eat; it''s a practical response to a genuinely fuller stretch. It''s common to find this transition harder than expected — asking for practical help specifically with your own meals is a reasonable request, not a failure. This week focuses on genuinely low-preparation meals, practical batch-cooking choices, and noticing what''s already easing rather than only what''s still hard.',
   'If you''re finding this stretch especially hard: it''s common, and worth saying out loud to your partner, a friend, or your own doctor — asking for help with meals or the household isn''t a failure, it''s exactly what this stretch calls for. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical — the A and B options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which fits your own targets rather than assuming either is interchangeable. If you''re still breastfeeding: your own energy and fluid needs continue through this stretch — the simple, low-preparation meals below still count as real nourishment. If low mood, persistent exhaustion beyond the expected tiredness, or a loss of interest in things you''d usually enjoy comes up during this adjustment, that''s worth mentioning to your doctor rather than assuming it''s just part of having two children now.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine — it''s the point this week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Caring for more than one child at once stretches your own eating thinner than a single-child stretch did — less time, less help sometimes, more happening at once. Leaning on low-preparation, batch-friendly meals this week isn''t a lesser way to eat; it''s a practical response to a genuinely fuller stretch.", "breakfast_a": "Overnight oats (1 cup, soaked in 150g curd) with a spoon of jaggery", "breakfast_b": "Peanut butter toast (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A big-batch dal (3/4 cup, cooked in bulk and reheated) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Pre-cooked chicken curry (100g chicken, reheated from a larger batch) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a portion of frozen mixed vegetables, heated through", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces, frozen and reheated) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Asking for help with your own meals isn''t a failure", "notes": "It''s common to find this transition harder than expected — and asking a partner, a relative or a friend for practical help specifically with your own meals is a reasonable, ordinary request, not a sign you''re not managing.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces, batter mixed the night before) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A boiled egg (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma curry (3/4 cup, made in a large batch) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "A one-pot dal-khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (made ahead and reheated) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A few weeks of simpler meals doesn''t undo your nutrition", "notes": "Repeating easy meals, eating in stages between feeds, or skipping the elaborate cooking you might normally do doesn''t erase your overall nutrition over a stretch like this. What matters more is getting enough across the day, however that actually happens right now.", "breakfast_a": "Ready-mix rava upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Muesli (1 cup) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Toor dal (3/4 cup, from a large batch) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Pre-marinated chicken tikka (100g chicken, oven-grilled) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a portion of frozen mixed vegetables, heated through", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, cooked in one pot) with curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken, reheated from a larger batch) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A predictable rhythm helps you too", "notes": "A simple, roughly consistent meal rhythm — even three basic points in the day — gives you one steady thing to count on amid a lot of household change. It doesn''t need to be elaborate or on the clock; roughly regular is enough.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces, batter made ahead) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup, batch-cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Pre-cooked chicken curry (100g chicken, reheated) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a quick cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces, frozen and reheated) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup, from a large batch)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Batch-friendly meals worth keeping ready", "notes": "A dal or curry made in a larger batch and portioned across a couple of days can be a genuine support right now, not a sign of cutting corners — freshly cooked and safely reheated food are equally real meals.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces, from batter kept ready in the fridge) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cheese-and-tomato sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup, batch-cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton, reheated from a larger batch) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "A one-pot masoor-methi dal-khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Noticing what''s easing, not just what''s still hard", "notes": "As the new routine becomes more familiar, your own eating tends to steady too, even if it doesn''t happen on a fixed schedule. Noticing which meals are already going more smoothly than a few weeks ago is a fair way to measure progress, alongside whatever still feels hard.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces, batter mixed ahead) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Pre-cooked chicken curry (100g chicken, reheated from a larger batch) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a quick cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces, frozen and reheated) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup, from a large batch)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there one small piece of help with your own meals — a batch-cooked dal, a family member picking up groceries — that would genuinely make this coming week easier?", "breakfast_a": "Overnight oats (1 cup, soaked in 150g curd) with a spoon of jaggery", "breakfast_b": "Peanut butter toast (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A big-batch dal (3/4 cup, cooked in bulk and reheated) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Pre-cooked chicken curry (100g chicken, reheated from a larger batch) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a portion of frozen mixed vegetables, heated through", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces, frozen and reheated) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to measure this stretch by how you''re managing now, rather than comparing it to how smoothly things ran with just one child?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: needing more support right now doesn''t undo how well you''ve managed so far.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 81: Eating what actually sounds good to you again (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   81,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating what actually sounds good to you again',
   '"Choosing a meal because you actually want it — not just because it''s fastest or whatever''s left — is a small thing worth reclaiming."',
   'After a long stretch where food decisions were mostly about speed, convenience, or what everyone else needed, it''s worth deliberately asking yourself what you''d actually enjoy eating — and choosing that, at least sometimes, rather than defaulting to whatever''s easiest without a thought. A bounded choice — deciding between two dishes you''d both actually enjoy — is enough to make a meal feel like yours again, without needing unlimited options or a lot of extra effort. This week focuses on reclaiming preference and appetite as your own, alongside the steady rhythm that''s already working for you.',
   'If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical — the A and B options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which fits your own targets. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you, even while choosing meals more by preference. If you''re still breastfeeding: your own appetite and energy needs continue through this stage — choosing meals you actually want doesn''t conflict with meeting them. A persistent loss of interest in eating, most meals feeling like a real struggle, or eating far less than usual for more than a couple of weeks is worth a conversation with your own doctor rather than assuming it will pass — seek help sooner if your intake has dropped substantially, you feel unwell, or you can''t manage your glucose within your prescribed plan.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "After a long stretch where food decisions were mostly about speed, convenience, or what everyone else needed, it''s worth deliberately asking yourself what you''d actually enjoy eating today — and choosing that, at least at one meal, rather than defaulting to whatever''s easiest.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed aloo paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cheese-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma-chawal — rajma curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Butter chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of fruit chaat with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Dal makhani (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer tikka masala (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A bounded choice is still a real choice", "notes": "You don''t need unlimited options to feel a genuine sense of choice — picking between two dishes you''d both actually enjoy, rather than accepting whatever''s quickest without a thought, is enough to make a meal feel like yours again.", "breakfast_a": "Masala dosa (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chole (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A plate of roasted makhana (30g) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Palak paneer (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Your appetite is allowed to vary too", "notes": "Hunger and fullness are useful information, worth listening to rather than overriding out of habit — some days a full plate feels right, other days less does. That''s a normal range to notice, but a marked or persistent drop in how much you''re eating still deserves attention rather than being assumed away.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A vegetable-and-cheese sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sambar (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Kadhi (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Saying ''not today'' to a dish is fine too", "notes": "You''re allowed to turn down a food you don''t feel like, even one you usually enjoy, without needing a reason beyond not wanting it today — the same way you''d extend that flexibility to anyone else.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Malai chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A steady rhythm still leaves room for preference", "notes": "Keeping roughly regular mealtimes doesn''t mean every meal has to look the same — the structure and the choice within it can both hold at once.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A vegetable-and-cheese omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Mutton rogan josh (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s more than a fluctuating appetite", "notes": "Wanting variety and having some ups and downs in appetite is normal. A persistent loss of interest in eating, most meals feeling like a real struggle, or eating far less than usual for more than a couple of weeks is different, and worth a conversation with your own doctor rather than waiting it out — seek help sooner if your intake has dropped substantially, you feel unwell, or you can''t manage your glucose within your prescribed plan.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Pre-cooked chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week you actually enjoyed most. Which meal this week did you choose because you actually wanted it, rather than because it was simply what was there?", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed aloo paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cheese-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma-chawal — rajma curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Butter chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of fruit chaat with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Dal makhani (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer tikka masala (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat your own food preferences as worth consulting, the same way you''d naturally check in with anyone else you were cooking for?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own preferences and appetite are worth listening to, not just managing around.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 82: Eating well while breastfeeding a toddler (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   82,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well while breastfeeding a toddler',
   '"Your nourishment still matters while breastfeeding continues — whatever your feeding pattern looks like."',
   'Major health organisations support continued breastfeeding alongside solid foods to two years or beyond when it remains mutually desired. There is no routine medical cutoff at which breastfeeding suddenly becomes inappropriate — the decision can remain with you and your child for as long as it continues to work for both of you. Nutritionally, breastmilk still contributes real value at this stage, even as solid food now provides most of your child''s calories and variety. Breastfeeding can continue to increase your own energy needs, though by how much varies with how often your child nurses, your activity level, body size and overall health — there''s no need to count calories rigidly, just eat regular, balanced meals and respond to your own hunger and fullness. Iron, calcium, iodine, choline, vitamin D and omega-3 fats are worth continued attention at this stage, and reliable vitamin B12 intake is essential if you eat no animal products — more on this later this week. Nursing patterns vary enormously at this stage — some mothers are down to brief, infrequent sessions, others are still nursing often, and both are ordinary. Whatever yours looks like, that alone doesn''t signal a problem or require you to make a decision about weaning.',
   'If your nursing pattern has changed recently: sessions can shift in number and timing for all sorts of reasons at this stage — becoming fewer, becoming more frequent for a while, or simply moving around the day. A change on its own, in either direction, isn''t a sign of anything going wrong. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this needs to be a bigger week than usual. Eat to your own hunger, and let nursing be whatever it is right now — no more, no less.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through. If you eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed is completely fine; you don''t need a new dish every day. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; a chilla, cheela or pesarattu is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week while breastfeeding, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re still nursing at this stage, that''s a normal continuation, not something you forgot to phase out — and it''s a good week to check in on your own eating, since it''s easy for your own meals to have quietly drifted while you''ve been focused on everyone else''s. Nursing doesn''t have to look a particular way by now to be working — there''s no single pattern every mother and child settle into, and yours doesn''t need to match anyone else''s.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable besan chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable besan chilla (2 pieces) with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with khichdi (1 heaped cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_a": "A chikoo with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A ripe pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mutton-vegetable keema curry (100g mutton)", "still_hungry": "Hummus (3 tbsp) with soft toast (2 slices) or vegetable sticks"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Eating for you, not a formula", "notes": "Breastfeeding can continue to increase your energy needs, but the amount varies with how frequently your child nurses, your activity level, body size and overall health. There is no need to count calories rigidly: eat regular, balanced meals, respond to hunger and fullness, and seek individual guidance if you are losing weight unintentionally, feeling persistently depleted or managing a medical condition. For you: Continue paying attention to iron, calcium, iodine, choline, vitamin D and omega-3 fats. If you eat no animal products, reliable vitamin B12 intake is essential because deficiency can affect both mother and breastfed child. Discuss an appropriate B12 source and whether you need iron, iodine, vitamin D, DHA or another supplement with a qualified healthcare professional.", "breakfast_a": "Sprouted-moong cheela (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Sprouted-moong cheela (2 pieces) with a boiled egg on the side and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rajma curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted almond powder (1 tbsp) stirred into curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Hummus (3 tbsp) spread on toast (2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a palak-paneer sabzi (75g paneer)", "dinner_b": "Roti (2 pieces) with a vegetable omelette (2 eggs)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a spoon of ground flaxseed"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Protecting your own routine, whatever nursing looks like for you", "notes": "There''s no single pattern nursing is supposed to settle into by now, and whatever yours looks like this week, it''s worth checking that your own meals and rest are protected around it — not squeezed into whatever time happens to be left over. Keep an easy nourishment option ready: curd with fruit, a fully cooked egg with toast and milk, or roasted chana with a calcium-fortified soy drink. Adjust the amount to your hunger; these can be a snack or part of a larger meal. Worth knowing: If you notice you''re skipping meals more often than not to fit around your day, that''s worth addressing directly — asking someone to take over for ten minutes at a specific mealtime, or keeping a simple backup meal ready, rather than assuming it will sort itself out.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge made with milk (1 cup cooked, 150ml milk), with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge made with milk (1 cup cooked, 150ml milk), with a fully cooked egg stirred in", "lunch_a": "Curd rice (1 cup, with 150g curd) with a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable curry (100g chicken) with curd rice (1 cup, with 150g curd)", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe mango with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Watermelon cubes (1 cup)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with an additional masoor dal (3/4 cup) on the side", "dinner_b": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lower-mercury fish curry (100g fish) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "Paneer (75g) with soft toast (2 slices)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "No expiry date on this", "notes": "There''s no medical cutoff where breastfeeding a toddler stops being appropriate — no age at which continuing suddenly becomes something to explain or defend. Whatever stage you''re at, your own meals still deserve the same attention they did earlier: an easy protein-and-calcium option on busy days, and portions that flex with your appetite rather than shrinking automatically. If mealtimes keep getting pushed later while you manage everything else, a simple plate you can make without much thought — dal, rice, a vegetable, curd — is worth keeping as a default rather than aiming for something more elaborate every day. If comments from others about nursing ''this long'' come your way, you don''t owe anyone an explanation — a short, deflecting answer or simply changing the subject works just as well as a justification.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu (2 pieces) with a mild chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Pesarattu (2 pieces) with a boiled egg on the side and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Bottle gourd and toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Sweet potato with a little roasted sesame (1 tsp) stirred in and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates blended into curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Paratha (1 medium) with a chana masala (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chicken-and-vegetable curry (100g chicken)", "still_hungry": "Hummus (3 tbsp) with soft toast (2 slices)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you''re feeling \"touched out\"", "notes": "Extended physical contact — nursing, carrying, someone climbing onto you the moment you sit down — can leave some mothers feeling touched-out: needing space from physical touch by the end of the day, even from a child you love nursing. That''s a common, normal response to sustained physical demand, not a sign you''re doing anything wrong or that you should stop. Naming it to yourself, and building in small windows without contact when you can — even a few minutes with a closed door — doesn''t take anything away from your child.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with grated vegetables and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with grated vegetables, a fully cooked egg on the side and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Green beans, potato and chana sabzi (3/4 cup chana) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a green beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe guava with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Cheese (30g) with toast (2 slices)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chicken-and-vegetable keema curry (100g chicken)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Good-to-know safety notes", "notes": "Many medicines are compatible with breastfeeding, but safety is specific to the medicine, dose and child. Check prescription medicines, over-the-counter products and herbal supplements with a doctor or pharmacist, or through a trusted lactation-medicine resource. Do not stop a necessary medicine or deliberately skip feeds without appropriate guidance. Alcohol and caffeine guidance hasn''t loosened either. If your own sessions happen to be occasional and fairly predictable right now, it may be easier to time an occasional drink around a gap in nursing than it was in the early months; if you''re still nursing frequently, the same spacing (roughly two hours per standard drink) applies regardless. Worth knowing: Occasional shorter, skipped or differently timed sessions can be normal when nursing a toddler. Because milk production responds to milk removal, a sustained reduction in nursing may gradually reduce supply. Seek support if the change is accompanied by breast pain, concern about your child''s fluid intake or growth, or an unwanted drop in milk production. For you: If you''re worried about your own milk supply or nutrition, raise it with your own doctor or a lactation consultant. If it''s your child''s growth or feeding that''s on your mind, their healthcare provider is the right person to ask. Either way, there''s no need to wait for a scheduled visit if something feels off.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup cooked) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma (1 cup cooked) with a boiled egg on the side and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Cabbage and peas curry with khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with khichdi (1 heaped cup) and a cabbage-peas sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Toast (2 slices) with almond butter (1 tbsp), mashed banana and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A ripe papaya with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a drumstick sabzi", "dinner_b": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a mutton curry (100g mutton) and a drumstick sabzi", "still_hungry": "Paneer (75g) with soft toast (2 slices)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. Which meal this week was easiest to protect, and could it become a default you lean on more often?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable besan chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable besan chilla (2 pieces) with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with khichdi (1 heaped cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_a": "A chikoo with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A ripe pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mutton-vegetable keema curry (100g mutton)", "still_hungry": "Hummus (3 tbsp) with soft toast (2 slices) or vegetable sticks"}]'::jsonb,
   'Which meal helped you feel comfortably nourished this week, and what would make that meal easier to repeat?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve you better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 83: Eating well in hot weather yourself (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   83,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well in hot weather yourself',
   '"Hot weather may change your appetite, but the essentials stay steady: drink water often, keep regular meals available, and pay attention to how you''re actually feeling."',
   'Use this week''s guidance whenever hot weather is actually happening where you are — if it''s currently mild or cool, continue with your usual rhythm and come back to this content the next time a heatwave rolls around. Heat changes the day for you too — smaller appetite, more sweating, and a bit more caution around food and fluids. If you''re breastfeeding, your fluid needs go up further still, so drinking proactively rather than waiting for thirst matters even more — unless you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction, in which case your prescriber''s guidance takes priority over the general advice below. This week focuses on staying ahead of hydration, eating what you can manage without forcing it, low-cooking-effort meals that are easy to keep safely stored in the heat, and the signs that heat is affecting you more than it should — including telling apart milder symptoms from a genuine heat emergency.',
   'If you manage diabetes: monitor your glucose as advised, maintain regular hydration unless you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction, and protect insulin, medicines, meters and test strips from direct heat — follow their storage instructions, and contact your care team if readings change significantly or you''re unable to eat or drink normally. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you, even with more buttermilk around in hot weather. If you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction: for a heart, kidney or other condition, follow your prescriber''s own fluid guidance in hot weather rather than the general "drink more" advice in this week. If you''re still breastfeeding: drink to thirst and keep fluids easily available — this supports how you feel, though drinking beyond that doesn''t reliably increase milk supply. A persistently reduced urine output (going noticeably less often, or dark urine), or a drop in milk output that continues for more than a day or two — especially alongside feeling unwell in the heat — is worth mentioning to your doctor. Confusion, collapse or suspected heat stroke is a medical emergency: call local emergency services immediately, move to a cooler place and begin cooling, and don''t give drinks to someone confused, unconscious or unable to swallow safely. Milder symptoms in someone alert — unusual drowsiness, a headache that won''t ease, feeling faint or unwell in the heat — still need prompt attention: move to a cooler place, sip fluids, and get medical help promptly rather than waiting to see if it passes.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, with extra care on refrigeration timing in the heat. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or your appetite is smaller than usual in the heat, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Use this week''s guidance whenever hot weather is actually happening where you are. Heat changes the day — smaller appetite, more sweating, a bit more caution needed around food and fluids, and more if you''re breastfeeding, since fluid needs go up further.", "breakfast_a": "Curd rice (1 cup, made with 150g curd) with a few curry leaves", "breakfast_b": "Chilled milk (200ml) with muesli (1 cup)", "lunch_a": "A quick pressure-cooker moong dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber salad", "lunch_b": "Tandoori chicken (100g chicken, cooked ahead and kept chilled until served) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "Watermelon cubes (chilled) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, quick one-pot) with curd", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Drink water proactively, not just when thirsty", "notes": "It''s easy to under-drink in heat, especially with a lot else going on — keep a water bottle within reach through the day and sip regularly rather than waiting to feel thirsty, unless you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction for a heart, kidney or other condition, in which case follow your prescriber''s fluid guidance instead. Water-rich foods like watermelon, cucumber and oranges are a helpful addition, not a replacement for drinking water itself.", "breakfast_a": "Chaat made with thoroughly cooked sprouts (1 cup), cooled promptly and refrigerated until served, with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold vegetable sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A quick sambar (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot salad", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "A quick masoor dal (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (quick and fully cooked) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg (kept chilled, peeled just before eating)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A smaller appetite in heat is common and usually temporary", "notes": "Eating less in hot weather is a commonly observed pattern and usually passes once the heat eases. Keep regular meals available and eat what you can manage rather than forcing a full plate — smaller, more frequent meals often sit better than three large ones in real heat.", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup, quick-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold fruit-and-curd bowl (150g curd with chopped seasonal fruit)", "lunch_a": "A quick toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "A chilled salad made with fully cooked chicken (100g), rice (1 cup cooked, cooled) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "A quick khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken, quick and fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Heat raises the food-safety stakes", "notes": "Refrigerate perishable food within two hours as usual — or within one hour once it''s been sitting above 32°C/90°F, tighter than the usual window. Low-cooking-effort dishes like curd rice, thoroughly cooked sprouts and chilled dals still need the same care once made: keep them refrigerated until served, and don''t let them sit out on a warm counter.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces, quick-steamed) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold boiled-egg-and-toast plate (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A quick masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, quick and fully cooked, kept chilled until reheated) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "A quick rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of chilled milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Replacing what the heat takes out", "notes": "Heavy sweating loses fluid and some salt together — but for ordinary heat exposure, your regular meals usually replace what''s lost, so there''s no need to routinely add extra salt to drinks. A home-style buttermilk alongside your regular meals is a reasonable, low-effort way to help with fluids. If you''re sweating heavily for a prolonged stretch — a long outdoor day, or an illness with fever in the heat — ask your doctor for individual guidance on replacing salt and fluid (CDC heat guidance).", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces, quick-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold paneer-and-vegetable sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A quick chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton, quick and fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "A quick masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s more than the heat", "notes": "Unusual drowsiness, a headache that won''t ease, or feeling faint or unwell in the heat needs prompt attention — don''t wait for hot, dry skin before taking it seriously, since heat stroke can occur even while still sweating. Confusion, collapse or suspected heat stroke is a step further than these milder symptoms and is a medical emergency. Worth knowing: Confusion, collapse or suspected heat stroke is an emergency: call local emergency services immediately, move to a cooler place and begin cooling (fan her, apply cool damp cloths to her skin). Do not give drinks to someone who is confused, unconscious, or unable to swallow safely. For milder heat symptoms in someone who is alert — feeling faint or unusually unwell — move to a cooler place, sip fluids, and get medical help promptly rather than waiting to see if it passes (CDC heat-illness guidance).", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces, quick-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "A cold cucumber-and-cheese sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, quick and fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "A quick chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Have you been drinking water proactively through the heat this week, or mostly only when you noticed you were thirsty?", "breakfast_a": "Curd rice (1 cup, made with 150g curd) with a few curry leaves", "breakfast_b": "Chilled milk (200ml) with muesli (1 cup)", "lunch_a": "A quick pressure-cooker moong dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber salad", "lunch_b": "Tandoori chicken (100g chicken, cooked ahead and kept chilled until served) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "Watermelon cubes (chilled) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, quick one-pot) with curd", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, quick-cooked) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat a smaller appetite in the heat as an ordinary, temporary adjustment rather than something to push through or worry about?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own comfort and safety in changing conditions deserve the same attention as anyone else''s.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 85: Eating well through a hungrier-than-usual stretch (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   85,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well through a hungrier-than-usual stretch',
   '"A hungrier stretch is usually just your own appetite swinging the other way — real and ordinary, and easy to meet with a bigger portion or a satisfying snack, familiar or new, whichever suits you."',
   'A run of days where you want more at every meal, or feel hungry sooner than usual, tends to happen every so often — ordinary day-to-day appetite variability running the hungrier direction rather than the smaller one. It calls for the same steady response either way: more of what''s already on offer. If you feel hungrier, you can increase a meal portion or add a satisfying snack — another helping of dal, rice, roti, fruit or curd, a packaged snack, or something sweet that appeals to you. Familiar meals and new choices can both fit without guilt; for lasting nourishment, it helps to regularly include protein and fibre, while choosing portions that suit your own appetite and any individual care plan you follow. A plate built around a grain, a dal or other legume, and dependable protein and calcium sources across the day already supports what a fuller stretch draws on, and pairing an iron-rich food with a vitamin-C food in the same meal helps your body absorb more of that iron. This week focuses on meeting a bigger appetite simply, and telling apart an ordinary hungrier stretch from something that needs your own doctor''s attention.',
   'If a bigger appetite comes with weight loss, unusual thirst or needing the bathroom much more often: that combination isn''t ordinary appetite variability — contact your own doctor promptly, ideally the same day, for advice and assessment. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual, and contact your care team if your glucose pattern or appetite changes noticeably — the A and B options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which fits your own targets. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements — a change in appetite is also worth mentioning at your next thyroid check. Vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid or deep breathing, unusually fruity-smelling breath, marked drowsiness or reduced responsiveness are signs that need emergency medical help immediately — call for emergency help rather than waiting to see if it settles, whether or not a bigger appetite has been part of the picture (NHS guidance on diabetic ketoacidosis).',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or your appetite is smaller than usual, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A run of days where you want more at every meal, or feel hungry sooner than usual, is a normal swing in appetite — not something to question or cut back on. If you feel hungrier, you can increase a meal portion or add a satisfying snack — another helping of dal, rice, roti, fruit or curd, or something new that appeals to you, whichever you''d actually enjoy.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo-methi paratha (2 pieces, extra ghee if you''d like) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Cheese-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked, extra helping available) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with roasted chana (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces, extra helping available) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a baingan bharta", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a baingan bharta", "still_hungry": "A second helping of whichever dish you chose, or a small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Familiar foods and new choices can both fit", "notes": "Familiar meals, packaged snacks and sweet foods can all fit without guilt. For lasting nourishment, regularly include protein and fibre, while choosing portions that suit your appetite and any individual care plan.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed gobi paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a ridge gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Punjabi-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dates (3-4 pieces)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg, or a second serving of dal and rice"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Pairing iron-rich food with vitamin C helps it go further", "notes": "A bigger appetite is a good moment to lean on a grain, a dal or other legume, and dependable protein and calcium sources across the day — a plate that already supports what a fuller stretch draws on. Pairing an iron-rich food with a vitamin-C food in the same meal (dal with a squeeze of lemon, or spinach with tomato) helps your body absorb more of that iron.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela, extra filling (2 pieces) with tamarind chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum paratha (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup, a squeeze of lemon) and a tinda sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tinda sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken tikka masala (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Steady or increasing energy needs nothing more than feeding it", "notes": "If a bigger appetite is coming alongside steady energy and you otherwise feel like yourself, it needs nothing more complicated than simply feeding it well. You can respond to hunger with a larger portion or a snack, while following any individual guidance on portions or carbohydrate intake.", "breakfast_a": "Thick daliya porridge (1 cup, cooked with milk) with roasted cashews (15g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach paratha (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup) and a karela sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-capsicum curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a karela sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted pistachios (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A hungrier week doesn''t mean a stricter one", "notes": "Meeting a bigger appetite with more regular food, rather than second-guessing whether you should be eating this much, keeps the week simple — there''s no need to restrict yourself because your hunger has picked up.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed paneer paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Thick masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-cabbage curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton rogan josh (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Amritsari fish (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s more than a hungrier stretch", "notes": "A bigger appetite that comes with weight loss, unusual thirst, needing the bathroom much more often, or increasing tiredness is a different picture from ordinary appetite variability — it''s worth contacting your own doctor promptly, ideally the same day, for advice and assessment, rather than putting it down to just being hungrier lately. Worth knowing: Vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid or deep breathing, unusually fruity-smelling breath, marked drowsiness or reduced responsiveness are signs that need emergency medical help immediately, whether or not a bigger appetite has been part of the picture — call for emergency help rather than waiting to see if it settles (NHS guidance on diabetic ketoacidosis).", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-stuffed paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, extra ghee if you''d like) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-peas curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Dates (3-4 pieces) with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. How did you end up meeting this week''s bigger appetite — bigger portions, familiar meals, something new, or a mix of all three?", "breakfast_a": "Aloo-methi paratha (2 pieces, extra ghee if you''d like) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Cheese-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked, extra helping available) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with roasted chana (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces, extra helping available) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a baingan bharta", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a baingan bharta", "still_hungry": "A second helping of whichever dish you chose, or a small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to trust a hungrier stretch as ordinary information from your own body, rather than something to question or manage down?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own appetite, whichever direction it swings, deserves a steady, unworried response.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
