-- postpartum week 86: Eating well when your own meals keep getting interrupted (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   86,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your own meals keep getting interrupted',
   '"A meal you finish in three sittings over an hour still counts as a meal — the interruption doesn''t erase what you managed to eat."',
   'At this stage, your own meals are often broken up by a mobile toddler who needs picking up, resettling or watching mid-bite. A meal eaten in three short sittings over an hour still counts as real nourishment — there''s no rule that says it has to be uninterrupted or finished in one sitting to matter. Choosing dishes that hold up reasonably well when they sit for a few minutes, and keeping a hand-held option ready for the days sitting down just isn''t realistic, both make an interrupted mealtime easier to work with rather than fight. This week focuses on working with a fragmented mealtime rather than treating it as a problem to fix, and noticing when it''s tipping into something worth a closer look.',
   'If mealtimes are consistently very stressful, or you''re regularly skipping meals altogether: rather than just delaying or fragmenting them, that''s worth raising with your own doctor or a trusted person. If you manage diabetes: follow your own individual plan for what to do when a meal is delayed or interrupted — this covers glucose monitoring and medication timing, not just food. If you use rapid-acting insulin, a delayed meal after a dose can risk hypoglycaemia, so check your specific guidance rather than assuming the usual schedule still applies unchanged (NHS guidance on rapid-acting insulin). If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you, even on a day your meals are eaten in stages. If you notice yourself feeling low or resentful around food most days, or eating has started to feel like a source of dread rather than an ordinary part of the day, that''s worth mentioning to your own doctor.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or your meal keeps getting interrupted, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Right now, your own meals are often broken up by a toddler who needs picking up, resettling or watching mid-bite — a few forkfuls, a pause, a few more. That''s a normal shape for a meal at this stage, not a failed one, and it''s worth eating this way without treating it as a problem to solve.", "breakfast_a": "A vegetable-stuffed paratha, cut into pieces (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-cheese sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A rajma-and-rice bowl (1 cup rice, 3/4 cup rajma, a cooked vegetable mixed in) — easy to eat in stages", "lunch_b": "A chicken-and-rice bowl (100g chicken, 1 cup rice, a cooked vegetable mixed in) — easy to eat in stages", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "A dal-stuffed paratha (2 pieces, about 4 tbsp dal filling total) with a small side salad and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "A paneer-and-vegetable roti roll (75g paneer, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A plate that survives being left and come back to", "notes": "Some dishes hold up better than others when your plate sits for a few minutes while you deal with something else — a dal-rice combination or a curry keeps its texture well when reheated, unlike something meant to be eaten hot and crisp straightaway. That''s a texture difference, not a food-safety one: if a meal gets interrupted, refrigerate whatever you''re not getting back to promptly, and treat it like any other perishable food — safe for up to two hours unrefrigerated (one hour above 32°C/90°F), reheated until steaming hot before you finish it.", "breakfast_a": "A moong dal cheela, cut into strips (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-vegetable wrap (2 eggs, 2 rotis) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A chana-and-rice bowl (1 cup rice, 3/4 cup chana curry, a cooked vegetable mixed in) — easy to eat in stages", "lunch_b": "A fish-and-rice bowl (100g fish, 1 cup rice, a cooked vegetable mixed in) — easy to eat in stages", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Vegetable cutlets (3 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "A chicken-and-vegetable roti roll (100g chicken, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A short, calm meal is still a real meal", "notes": "There''s no minimum length a meal needs to run for to count — a genuinely short but calm stretch of eating gives you real nourishment, the same as a longer, uninterrupted one would. It''s the food eaten, not the time spent at the table, that matters.", "breakfast_a": "A besan chilla, cut into strips (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-cheese wrap (2 eggs, 2 rotis) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A toor dal-and-rice bowl (1 cup rice, 3/4 cup toor dal, a cooked vegetable mixed in) — easy to eat in stages", "lunch_b": "A chicken tikka roll (100g chicken, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "A paneer-stuffed paratha (2 pieces, about 50g paneer filling total) with a small side salad", "dinner_b": "A mutton keema paratha (2 pieces, 75g mutton keema) with a small side salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Asking for ten covered minutes is a reasonable ask", "notes": "Where it''s possible, asking a partner or another adult in the house to take over for the ten or fifteen minutes your meal actually needs isn''t a big favour to request — it''s a reasonable, practical ask that makes an uninterrupted meal an occasional option, not something to feel you shouldn''t need.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, wrapped in a roti (2 eggs, 1 roti) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A hand-held option for the days it''s really not going to happen", "notes": "On days when sitting down at all just isn''t realistic, a roti roll or a sandwich you can eat one-handed while staying on your feet still counts as a real meal — it isn''t a lesser substitute, just a different shape for a genuinely busy day.", "breakfast_a": "A besan chilla roll with vegetables (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-spinach wrap (2 eggs, 2 rotis) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A vegetable-and-paneer roti roll (75g paneer, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "lunch_b": "A fish tikka roll (100g fish, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "A chana-stuffed paratha (2 pieces, about 4 tbsp chana filling total) with a small side salad and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "A mutton-and-vegetable roti roll (100g mutton, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s worth a closer look", "notes": "An occasionally interrupted meal is ordinary. If mealtimes are consistently very stressful for you, you''re regularly skipping meals altogether rather than just delaying or fragmenting them, or you notice yourself feeling low or resentful around food most days, that''s worth raising with your own doctor or a trusted person rather than assuming it will simply sort itself out.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia with roasted nuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "A tandoori chicken roll (100g chicken, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "An egg-and-pea curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did you manage to eat a full meal''s worth of food this week, even if it happened across several short sittings rather than one sit-down stretch?", "breakfast_a": "A vegetable-stuffed paratha, cut into pieces (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-cheese sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "A rajma-and-rice bowl (1 cup rice, 3/4 cup rajma, a cooked vegetable mixed in) — easy to eat in stages", "lunch_b": "A chicken-and-rice bowl (100g chicken, 1 cup rice, a cooked vegetable mixed in) — easy to eat in stages", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "A dal-stuffed paratha (2 pieces, about 4 tbsp dal filling total) with a small side salad and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "A paneer-and-vegetable roti roll (75g paneer, 2 rotis) — easy to eat one-handed", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to count a meal by whether you actually ate, rather than by whether it looked the way a meal is supposed to look?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: an interrupted, imperfect version of something still counts as the real thing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 87: Retiring your postpartum recovery foods, at your own pace (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   87,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Retiring your postpartum recovery foods, at your own pace',
   '"There''s no single day this needs to happen — a steady, unhurried shift away from the specific foods that carried you through early recovery, on whatever timeline suits you, doesn''t undo any of the good they did."',
   'Many families keep a specific postpartum recovery diet going well past the early weeks it was really meant for — a daily laddoo, extra ghee stirred into everything, a familiar dal made a particular way. There''s no single date this needs to stop, and taking your own time with it doesn''t mean anything has gone wrong. If a particular food is something you''d genuinely choose anyway, keep it — that''s simply enjoying a food you like, not still being in recovery. If something has started to feel like an obligation rather than a want, that''s a fair signal to quietly let it go. Any prescribed medicine or supplement you take is a separate decision from all of this — continue it exactly as directed regardless of which ordinary foods you keep or retire. An herbal water, tonic, or specific preparation someone recommended sits in a different category again — check its safety with a clinician or pharmacist rather than deciding it the same way you''d decide about an ordinary food you enjoy. This week focuses on noticing which recovery-period habits still serve you and which don''t, without pressure to decide all at once.',
   'If you''re taking a prescribed medicine or supplement: continue it exactly as directed — that''s a separate decision from anything in this week''s food choices, whatever you keep or retire. Ordinary recovery-period foods — a laddoo, extra ghee, a familiar dal: can be changed, reduced or stopped freely, at whatever pace suits you; there''s no need to check with anyone before adjusting an ordinary food. If you''re taking an herbal water, tonic or other herbal remedy someone recommended: check its safety with a clinician or pharmacist, especially alongside any medication or while breastfeeding. Continue any prescribed treatment as directed — this is a different decision from an ordinary food you simply enjoy. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — moving away from a recovery-specific dish (often sweetened or ghee-heavy) is a reasonable point to also check it against your own targets. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements, regardless of which other foods you keep or retire. If letting go of a recovery-period habit brings up guilt or pressure that feels hard to shake, or a family member''s insistence is becoming a genuine source of stress, that''s worth talking through with your own doctor or a trusted person.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''re easing away from a recovery-specific dish, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Many mothers keep a specific recovery diet going for a while after the early weeks it was really meant for — a daily laddoo, extra ghee in everything, a familiar dal made a particular way. There''s no fixed date this needs to stop. If you''re ready to let some of it go, or want to keep the parts you still genuinely enjoy, both are completely fine. An herbal water, tonic, or specific preparation someone recommended is a different category — check its safety with a clinician or pharmacist, rather than deciding it the same way you''d decide about a laddoo. Continue any prescribed treatment as directed.", "breakfast_a": "Luchi (2 pieces) with aloo dum and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a cholar dal (3/4 cup) and a shukto", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a shukto", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of sweetened milk (200ml) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a potol sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-potol curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Keep what you still enjoy, not out of obligation", "notes": "If a particular recovery-period food — a laddoo, a spiced milk, a specific dal — is something you''d choose anyway because you like it, there''s no reason to stop just because the early weeks it was ''for'' have passed. Eating it now is simply eating something you enjoy, not still being in recovery.", "breakfast_a": "Chirer pulao, flattened-rice pulao (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lau sabzi", "lunch_b": "Shorshe maach, mustard fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lau sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dates (3-4 pieces)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, Bengali-style (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Letting go of what feels like a chore now", "notes": "If a recovery-period food or ritual has started to feel like an obligation rather than something you actually want, that''s a reasonable signal to quietly stop — nothing about your recovery unravels because a specific dish stops appearing on your plate.", "breakfast_a": "Suji upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a cholar dal (3/4 cup) and a jhinge sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, jhol-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a jhinge sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-tomato dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry, kosha mangsho (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "An ordinary, balanced plate covers you just as well", "notes": "You don''t need a special recovery dish. Varied everyday meals — a grain, a dal or other protein, a vegetable, and dairy at more than one meal — can support your nourishment once you''re well past the early weeks, while any prescribed treatment or supplements you take remain separate from whichever ordinary foods you choose to keep.", "breakfast_a": "Dhokla (3 pieces) with green chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Family pressure to keep going is worth a gentle word", "notes": "If a family member keeps pressing a recovery-specific food or ritual on you well past when it was meant to run, a simple, non-defensive line — that you''re doing well now and eating your usual meals — is enough; you don''t owe a longer explanation for a personal decision about your own plate.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-cabbage curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Tandoori fish (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Taking longer than expected is fine too", "notes": "If you''re not quite ready to let go of a recovery-period habit yet, or you find yourself going back to one occasionally, that''s not a step backward — there''s no deadline attached to any of this, and the pace that suits you is the right one.", "breakfast_a": "Chire bhaja, roasted flattened rice (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a cholar dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-carrot dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there a recovery-period food or habit you''re still keeping mostly out of habit, that you''d be fine letting go of — and one you''d genuinely miss and want to keep?", "breakfast_a": "Luchi (2 pieces) with aloo dum and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a cholar dal (3/4 cup) and a shukto", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a shukto", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of sweetened milk (200ml) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a potol sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-potol curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to choose your own pace for this, the way you''d want anyone else''s transition respected on their own timeline?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own eating gets to keep evolving, on no one''s schedule but yours.')
