-- postpartum week 88: Rethinking salt and sugar in your own everyday food (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   88,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Rethinking salt and sugar in your own everyday food',
   '"Your own food doesn''t need extra salt or sugar to taste good to you either — small, steady adjustments this week are enough, no all-at-once overhaul required."',
   'Added salt and sugar often show up quietly in an ordinary day — a spoon stirred into chai out of habit, a packaged snack reached for without checking the label, a heavily salted home-cooked curry. This week is about noticing where they show up for you specifically, not an all-at-once overhaul. For salt, the total amount across the day is what matters — frequent snacking can push that total higher simply by adding more food, but eating the same amount at fewer sittings doesn''t reduce it. Home cooking contributes just as easily as packaged food does. Frequency matters separately for dental health: sweet foods and drinks sipped or snacked on continuously give your teeth more acid exposures through the day, regardless of the total amount. This week focuses on small, steady adjustments — tasting before adding more salt, easing sugar down gradually, checking labels on the packaged foods you reach for often — rather than a strict rule to follow perfectly.',
   'If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the specific limits advised for you — this week''s general noticing doesn''t replace guidance you''ve already been given. If you manage diabetes: continue your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule as usual — the A and B options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which fits your own targets, and reducing added sugar elsewhere in the day doesn''t change that. If you''re prone to dental sensitivity or cavities: spacing out sweet foods and drinks to set points in the day, rather than sipping or snacking on them continuously, gives your teeth fewer acid exposures through the day. If cutting back on salt or sugar starts to feel like a strict rule you''re anxious about breaking, that''s worth easing back from — the aim is a gradual, comfortable shift, not a source of stress.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Added salt and sugar often show up quietly — a spoon stirred into chai out of habit, a heavier hand with the salt shaker at the table, a packaged snack reached for without checking the label. This week is about simply noticing where they show up in your own day, not cutting everything out at once.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia, lightly salted (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Herb-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lightly-salted moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Lemon-pepper chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g, unsalted) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd), no added salt", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal, tempered with cumin (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Grilled paneer (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The total across the day is what counts", "notes": "For salt, it''s the total amount across the day that matters, not how it''s spread out — eating the same amount at fewer sittings doesn''t reduce it. Frequent snacking can push the total higher simply by adding more food, so a useful habit is noticing how many salty extras add up over a day, rather than assuming that spacing them out makes a difference on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla, herb-forward (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with coriander, a pinch of salt (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry, garlic-forward (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled fish with lemon and herbs (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g, unsalted) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal, tempered with cumin (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry, lightly salted (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Home cooking adds up just as easily as packaged food", "notes": "A heavily salted curry, dal or chutney made at home contributes just as much salt as a packaged snack does — it''s not only about what comes in a wrapper. Tasting before adding a second round of salt, and letting spices and aromatics carry more of the flavour, keeps a dish satisfying without relying as heavily on the salt itself.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa (2 pieces) with a low-salt tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal, tempered with cumin and garlic rather than extra salt (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Tandoori chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g, unsalted) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry, lightly salted (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry, modest salt (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Checking labels on the foods you reach for often", "notes": "A packaged snack, sauce, pickle or namkeen you eat regularly is worth a label check — sodium content varies a great deal between similar-looking products, and a small swap toward a lower-sodium version of something you already like is an easy change that doesn''t ask you to give the food up.", "breakfast_a": "Steamed dhokla, less-sweet batter (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Baked chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g, unsalted) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry, lightly salted (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry, modest salt (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A gradually less sweet cup of chai", "notes": "If your chai carries more sugar than you''d choose if you thought about it, easing the amount down gradually over a couple of weeks — rather than switching all at once — usually feels far less noticeable than it sounds, and your palate adjusts faster than expected.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idiyappam, steamed rice noodles (1 cup) with a low-salt coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry, lightly salted (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Grilled mutton chops (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g, unsalted) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal, tempered with cumin (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Steamed fish with ginger and lemon (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "This is a direction to move in, not a strict rule", "notes": "None of this needs to be perfect or immediate — noticing where salt and sugar quietly add up, and making small, steady adjustments where they''re easy, is the whole point. An occasional heavily salted or sweet dish you enjoy doesn''t undo the rest of the week.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with a low-salt tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi, lightly salted (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g, unsalted) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry, lightly salted (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry, modest salt (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did you notice one place this week where salt or sugar was showing up more out of habit than actual craving?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia, lightly salted (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Herb-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lightly-salted moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Lemon-pepper chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g, unsalted) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd), no added salt", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal, tempered with cumin (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Grilled paneer (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to make one small, steady adjustment this week and let that be enough, rather than aiming to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments to your own everyday food add up more than any single overhaul would.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 89: Making sure you get enough vitamin D too (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   89,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Making sure you get enough vitamin D too',
   '"Your own bone health deserves attention, whether you breastfeed or not. This week looks at vitamin D food sources and applicable supplement guidance."',
   'Vitamin D supports calcium absorption, bone health and immune function. Low levels are common, especially with limited sun exposure, darker skin, covered clothing, or time spent mostly indoors — all common in the postpartum months, when many mothers are indoors more and outside less than usual. Very few everyday foods carry meaningful vitamin D — egg yolks, fatty fish such as salmon, sardines and Atlantic mackerel (not king mackerel, which is high in mercury), and vitamin-D-fortified milk are the useful ones. Food sources alone usually aren''t enough to meet the full requirement, which is part of why this deserves attention rather than being left unaddressed. Your own vitamin D needs don''t end when breastfeeding does, and they aren''t covered by your baby''s own separate supplement — the two are managed independently, whatever stage of feeding you''re at.',
   'If you have a history of kidney stones or a kidney condition: check with your own clinician before starting any vitamin D supplement, since it affects how your body handles calcium. If you take medication for epilepsy, tuberculosis, or certain other long-term conditions: some medications affect vitamin D metabolism — mention this if a supplement is being considered. Vitamin D needs deserve attention, but not everyone needs a blood test. Follow whatever routine supplement guidance applies to you; testing, or a higher treatment-level dose, may be appropriate for specific medical reasons your clinician identifies, rather than something to start on your own (Endocrine Society guideline on vitamin D).',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, and fatty fish and egg yolks are also useful vitamin D sources. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin D supports calcium absorption, bone health and immune function, and low levels are common — especially with limited sun exposure, darker skin, covered clothing, or time spent mostly indoors, all of which are common in the postpartum months. Food alone rarely covers the full requirement, which is part of why this is worth a specific conversation with your own clinician rather than something to guess at.", "breakfast_a": "Besan-and-vegetable chilla (2 pieces) with a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml, check the label)", "breakfast_b": "Mushroom-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A handful of food sources actually help", "notes": "Very few everyday foods carry meaningful vitamin D, but egg yolks, fatty fish such as salmon, sardines and Atlantic mackerel (not king mackerel, which is high in mercury), and vitamin-D-fortified milk are the useful ones — worth including regularly rather than as a rare treat, even though food alone usually isn''t enough on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled sardines (100g) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml) with a fig", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Food and supplement sources are the reliable route", "notes": "Some sunlight exposure lets your body make its own vitamin D, but the amount needed to matter and the skin-cancer risk of getting it both vary too much by skin tone, season and location to turn into a specific instruction here — dermatology guidance generally recommends sun protection rather than deliberately unprotected exposure (American Academy of Dermatology). Food sources and, if your clinician recommends it, a supplement are the more dependable way to cover this, with sunlight as a bonus rather than the plan.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coconut-based (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Your own needs don''t end when breastfeeding does", "notes": "Vitamin D matters for your own bone health and immune function whether you''re currently breastfeeding, have weaned, or never breastfed at all — this isn''t only a pregnancy-and-breastfeeding-window need.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Atlantic mackerel curry (100g Atlantic mackerel — not king mackerel, which is high in mercury) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-methi curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Attention doesn''t have to mean testing", "notes": "Vitamin D needs deserve attention, but that doesn''t mean everyone needs a blood test — routine supplement guidance, where it applies to you, is often enough on its own. A test becomes useful for a specific medical reason: symptoms your clinician wants to investigate, a known risk factor, or before starting a higher, treatment-level dose rather than a routine one.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Salmon-and-spinach curry (100g salmon) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "This is worth raising, not managing alone", "notes": "Follow whatever routine vitamin D supplement guidance applies to you, and don''t self-start a high-dose or treatment-level supplement on your own — those doses are meant to correct a specific, confirmed deficiency and aren''t the same as an ordinary daily amount. If you''re unsure what applies to you, that''s a reasonable thing to ask your clinician at your next visit rather than deciding alone.", "breakfast_a": "Poha with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-tomato curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Do you know whether any routine vitamin D supplement guidance applies to you? If you''re not sure, that''s worth asking about at your next visit.", "breakfast_a": "Besan-and-vegetable chilla (2 pieces) with a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml, check the label)", "breakfast_b": "Mushroom-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a small, regular habit — checking a fortified-food label, a fatty-fish dinner once or twice a week — that would be easy to build in without much extra effort?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — this time, revisiting a food you might have been holding back on without needing to.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 90: Eating spicy food again, if that's what you enjoy (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   90,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating spicy food again, if that''s what you enjoy',
   '"There''s no blanket rule that breastfeeding means giving up spicy food. Choose the level you enjoy and tolerate."',
   'This week is about choosing flavours you enjoy, without unnecessary food rules. Adjust spice to your own comfort; there is no need to increase the heat or eat bland food to follow this chart. Separately from your baby, spicy food that leaves you with heartburn or reflux is worth moderating for your own comfort, whatever the reason it''s happening — that''s a reason grounded in how you feel, not a rule about breastfeeding itself.',
   'If you have gastritis, acid reflux, or a history of stomach ulcers: continue following whatever level of spice your own condition and treatment plan call for — this week''s general permission doesn''t override guidance you''ve already been given for a diagnosed condition. If you''re following a clinician-directed elimination diet for your baby''s diagnosed condition: continue following that specific plan — this week''s general permission is about spice level, not about foods you''ve already been told to avoid for a diagnosed reason.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, spiced to your own taste. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. "Spicy" and "spiced to taste" throughout mean your own comfortable level, not a fixed amount — adjust freely up or down. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A common piece of advice tells breastfeeding mothers to avoid spicy food altogether, but there''s no general reason to — choose the level of spice you personally enjoy and tolerate, the same as you would outside pregnancy or breastfeeding.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha, made spicy to taste (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with green chilli (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spicy rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Spicy chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with roasted cumin and a pinch of black pepper", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spicy chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Spicy paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Chilli heat and other spices aren''t the same thing", "notes": "Spice isn''t a single dial. Chilli is what brings the heat, while other spices — jeera, garam masala, ginger, black pepper — add flavour without necessarily adding heat. If you want a well-spiced dish with less heat, it''s the chilli you can scale back rather than the spice level as a whole.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla with green chilli (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji with chilli (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spicy moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Spicy fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spicy masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Spicy egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Your own spice tolerance is worth trusting too", "notes": "How much spice you personally enjoy is worth trusting on its own terms — there''s no need to match what anyone else at the table eats, or push yourself hotter or milder than what actually tastes good to you. If a dish that used to feel comfortable now feels like too much, or you find yourself wanting more heat than before, that''s just your own taste changing, not something to second-guess.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with spicy tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast with chilli flakes (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spicy toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken chettinad (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spicy chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Spicy chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Your own digestion is worth listening to as well", "notes": "Separately from your baby, spicy food that leaves you with heartburn or reflux is worth moderating for your own comfort, whatever the reason it''s happening — that''s a reason grounded in how you feel, not a rule about breastfeeding itself.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with spicy sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast with chilli flakes (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spicy masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken kolhapuri (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spicy rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Spicy paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Building back up is a fine approach too", "notes": "If you deliberately ate blander food during pregnancy or early postpartum and want to ease back toward your usual level of spice, there''s no need to do it all at once — a gradual return over a couple of weeks works just as well and lets you notice your own comfort along the way.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with spicy coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Spicy egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spicy chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with green chilli (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spicy masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Mutton rogan josh (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "This is genuinely your call to make", "notes": "If people around you insist spicy food is off-limits while breastfeeding, you can hold your ground without much explanation needed — this is a widely repeated idea without strong general evidence behind it, and your own comfort is a better guide than the blanket version of the rule.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with spicy tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion toast with green chilli (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Spicy khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken vindaloo (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spicy chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Spicy paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Which meals felt enjoyable and comfortable for you this week?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha, made spicy to taste (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with green chilli (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spicy rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Spicy chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with roasted cumin and a pinch of black pepper", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spicy chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Spicy paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a food you''ve been quietly avoiding because of a general rule you were told, rather than your own experience with it?',
   'This closes this stretch of weekly themes. Whatever comes next, the same approach carries forward: your own appetite, comfort and preferences are a legitimate starting point, not something to override by default.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 91: Understanding fat in your own diet (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   91,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Understanding fat in your own diet',
   '"Fat is an important nutrient. Prefer unsaturated sources such as suitable vegetable oils, nuts and seeds; ghee can fit in modest amounts. Limit saturated fat and avoid industrial trans fat, rather than avoiding all fat."',
   'Fat carries energy, helps you absorb vitamins A, D, E and K, and supports hormone production — needs that apply whether or not you''re currently breastfeeding, though your energy needs run somewhat higher than usual while you are, which makes fat a nutrient worth including normally rather than restricting. Fat is an important nutrient overall. Prefer unsaturated sources — vegetable oils such as mustard, groundnut or sunflower, nuts and seeds — with ghee fitting in as a modest amount rather than the dominant fat in your day. Limit saturated fat, most concentrated in ghee, butter and fatty cuts of meat — this applies generally, not only if you have diagnosed high cholesterol — and avoid industrial trans fat, found in vanaspati (hydrogenated vegetable shortening) and some packaged fried or bakery snacks. Oil reused repeatedly for frying is worth avoiding separately from trans fat: repeated heating breaks any oil down, regardless of type. Walnuts, flaxseed and mustard oil provide a plant form of omega-3 (ALA) that your body converts only partially into the DHA and EPA found directly in oily fish — worth including both if fish is part of your diet. This week focuses on choosing fat sources thoughtfully — favouring unsaturated fat, limiting saturated fat, and avoiding trans fat and repeatedly reused frying oil — rather than avoiding fat altogether, and noticing when fat guidance for you specifically differs from the everyday default.',
   'If you have gallbladder disease, pancreatitis, or a diagnosed malabsorption condition: follow whatever fat guidance your own care team has given you rather than this week''s general framing. If you have high cholesterol or a cardiovascular condition: continue following your own prescribed dietary guidance on saturated fat and cooking oil choices — this week''s general framing on limiting saturated fat doesn''t override an individual treatment plan. Persistent greasy or pale stools, unexplained weight loss, or ongoing abdominal pain are worth raising with your own doctor rather than adjusting fat intake on your own without knowing the cause. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s fat, sodium or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Rajasthani cooking (gatte ki sabzi, ker-sangri, panchmel dal, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Fat carries energy, helps you absorb vitamins A, D, E and K, and supports hormone production — needs that apply whether or not you''re currently breastfeeding, and run somewhat higher than usual if you are. There''s no general reason to cut fat back, though choosing more unsaturated fat and less saturated or trans fat still matters.", "breakfast_a": "Besan gatte ki sabzi (3/4 cup) with missi roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs, cooked in 1 tsp ghee) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ker-sangri sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ker-sangri sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted flaxseed (1 tsp, ground)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a panchmel dal (3/4 cup, tempered in 1 tsp ghee)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Saturated and trans fat are worth limiting — not fat as a whole", "notes": "Saturated fat — concentrated in ghee, butter, and fatty cuts of meat — is worth keeping to a modest share of your day''s fat rather than the dominant share, and this applies generally, not only if you have diagnosed high cholesterol. Industrial trans fat — found in vanaspati (hydrogenated vegetable shortening) and some packaged fried or bakery snacks — is worth avoiding altogether. Oil that''s been reused repeatedly for frying is worth avoiding too, as a separate concern from trans fat: repeated heating breaks oil down regardless of the oil type. None of this means avoiding fat broadly — unsaturated oils, nuts, seeds and ghee in modest amounts still belong in your everyday meals.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha, made with mustard oil (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs, cooked in 1 tsp oil) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Guar phali sabzi (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish, cooked in mustard oil) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a guar phali sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Plant omega-3 and fish omega-3 aren''t quite the same thing", "notes": "Walnuts, flaxseed and mustard oil provide a plant form of omega-3 (ALA), which your body converts only partially into the DHA and EPA forms found directly in oily fish — worth including both if you eat fish, rather than treating plant sources as a full substitute for it.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tinda sabzi (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a toor dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tinda sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted flaxseed (1 tsp, ground) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Plain lower-fat dairy is a fine choice — packaged low-fat products need a closer look", "notes": "Plain lower-fat milk or curd can be a perfectly suitable choice, not just a compromise — the main difference is fat content, not nutritional quality. For packaged ''low-fat'' products, it''s worth comparing the full ingredient list rather than assuming either the regular or low-fat version is automatically better: some low-fat packaged foods add sugar or starch to make up for the texture fat would otherwise provide, but that varies by product rather than being a fixed rule.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar (1 tsp oil tempering) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Lobia curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a baingan bharta", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-capsicum curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a baingan bharta", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Cooking fat counts too — not just what''s added afterwards", "notes": "The ghee or oil used while cooking counts toward your day''s fat intake just as much as anything added afterwards — it isn''t exempt just because it went into the pan rather than onto the plate. Being mindful of how much ghee or oil goes into a dish while it''s cooking, alongside any extra spooned on top afterwards, is what actually adds up over a day.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed paratha (2 pieces, cooked in 1 tsp ghee) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, usual amount of ghee) with curd", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a turai sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Dates (3-4 pieces) with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Grilled fish (100g fish, brushed with 1 tsp oil) with roti (2 pieces) and a turai sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When fat guidance genuinely differs for you", "notes": "If you have a diagnosed condition affecting fat digestion or absorption — gallbladder disease, pancreatitis, a malabsorption condition — or persistent greasy or pale stools, unexplained weight loss, or ongoing abdominal pain, that''s worth raising with your own doctor rather than adjusting fat intake on your own without knowing the cause.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup, 1 tsp ghee) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mint toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gobi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gobi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a piece of gur (jaggery)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did this week change how you think about fat — as something to choose thoughtfully, rather than something to minimize across the board?", "breakfast_a": "Besan gatte ki sabzi (3/4 cup) with missi roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs, cooked in 1 tsp ghee) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ker-sangri sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ker-sangri sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted flaxseed (1 tsp, ground)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a panchmel dal (3/4 cup, tempered in 1 tsp ghee)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a specific fat source — ghee, oil, nuts, fish — you''ve been quietly avoiding out of habit, rather than for any reason that actually applies to you?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — your own fluid needs.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 92: Your own hydration and fluid needs (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   92,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Your own hydration and fluid needs',
   '"Thirst and pale urine are a better everyday guide than a fixed number of glasses — and drinking beyond thirst doesn''t increase milk supply."',
   'Thirst, and urine that runs pale straw-coloured rather than dark, are a practical everyday guide to whether you''re drinking enough — more useful day to day than counting a fixed number of glasses. Thirst is a useful guide for many people; drink regularly through the day and adjust upward for heat, activity and illness rather than relying on thirst alone in those situations. If you''re currently breastfeeding, your fluid needs run somewhat higher than before. Deliberately drinking well beyond thirst is a common piece of advice for increasing milk supply, but it doesn''t work that way — supply responds mainly to how often and how effectively milk is removed, not to extra water. Soups, dal, curd, buttermilk, tea, coffee and water-rich fruit and vegetables all count toward your day''s fluid total too, not just plain water. This week focuses on hydration as a matter of thirst and everyday variety, rather than a number to chase — and on when it''s genuinely worth drinking more deliberately, or when it''s worth getting help.',
   'If you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction: for a heart, kidney or other condition, follow your own care team''s specific fluid guidance rather than this week''s general framing. If you have a fever, are vomiting, or have diarrhoea: your fluid needs rise further — sip fluids regularly, consider oral rehydration solution prepared per label alongside food (not instead of it), and contact your own doctor if you can''t keep fluids down. Persistent dizziness on standing, markedly reduced urination, or being unable to keep fluids down at all needs urgent medical advice, not just a mention at your next routine visit — contact your own doctor promptly if any of these happen. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s fluid, sodium or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Gujarati and Kathiyawadi cooking (thepla, dhokla, undhiyu, fajeto, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Thirst and pale straw-coloured urine are useful everyday guides for many people. Drink regularly and adjust for heat, activity and illness. Follow any prescribed fluid restriction. If you''re currently breastfeeding, your fluid needs run somewhat higher than before.", "breakfast_a": "Methi thepla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-fenugreek bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gujarati kadhi (1 bowl) with khichdi (1 cup) and an undhiyu", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an undhiyu", "nourishment_break_a": "A watermelon slice with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki muthiya curry (3/4 cup) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "More water doesn''t mean more milk", "notes": "Deliberately drinking well beyond thirst is a common piece of advice for increasing milk supply, but it doesn''t work that way — milk supply responds mainly to how often and how effectively milk is removed, not to extra water. Staying adequately hydrated matters for you regardless, just not as a supply lever.", "breakfast_a": "Khaman dhokla (3 pieces) with green chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a gujarati kadhi-dal (3/4 cup) and a sev-tameta sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sev-tameta sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A cucumber-mint salad with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Coconut water (200ml) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Khakhra (3 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with khakhra (3 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fluids come from more than what you drink", "notes": "Soups, dal, curd, buttermilk, and water-rich fruit and vegetables like watermelon, cucumber and oranges all count toward your day''s fluid total — not just plain water. A varied day''s meals already contribute a meaningful share.", "breakfast_a": "Handvo (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup) and a cucumber raita", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cucumber raita", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted pistachios (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a piece of jaggery-sesame chikki", "dinner_a": "A clear vegetable-and-dal soup (1 bowl, made with 1/2 cup cooked dal) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Tea and coffee count toward your fluid intake", "notes": "Tea and coffee count towards your fluid intake, the same as water does — there''s no need to subtract part of their volume when thinking about hydration. Keep total caffeine within the guidance that applies to you — under 200mg a day if you''re breastfeeding or could be pregnant.", "breakfast_a": "Rava idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gathiya nu shaak (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a toor dal", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gathiya nu shaak", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of ajwain", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-fenugreek curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Heat, activity and a fever all raise your needs", "notes": "Hot weather, more physical activity than usual, or a fever all increase how much fluid you need beyond an ordinary day — worth drinking more deliberately at those times rather than sticking to your usual routine amount.", "breakfast_a": "Dabeli (1 piece, whole-wheat bun) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Fajeto (raw-mango kadhi, 3/4 cup, made with 1/2 cup curd) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of nimbu pani (lightly salted, 200ml) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a sprig of curry leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When your own fluid guidance genuinely differs", "notes": "If you''re following a prescribed fluid restriction for a heart, kidney or other condition, follow your own care team''s fluid guidance rather than this week''s general framing — ''drink to thirst'' doesn''t apply the same way when intake itself needs to stay within a set limit.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-stuffed muthiya (3 pieces, steamed) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-methi toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an undhiyu-style mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and a moong-masoor dal", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an undhiyu-style mixed-vegetable curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square (jaggery-sesame, 1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Khakhra (3 pieces) with a lobia-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with khakhra (3 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did paying attention to thirst this week change how much, or how little, you actually drank compared with what you expected?", "breakfast_a": "Methi thepla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-fenugreek bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gujarati kadhi (1 bowl) with khichdi (1 cup) and an undhiyu", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an undhiyu", "nourishment_break_a": "A watermelon slice with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki muthiya curry (3/4 cup) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a fluid habit you''ve kept out of a rule you were told — drinking well past thirst, for instance — that''s worth checking against how you actually feel instead?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — fibre in your own meals.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 93: Getting enough fibre in your everyday meals (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   93,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Getting enough fibre in your everyday meals',
   '"A steady mix of whole grains, dal, vegetables and fruit gets you there — fibre is a routine, everyday target, not something to chase only once things feel backed up."',
   'Whole grains, dal and other legumes, vegetables, fruit, nuts and seeds all contribute fibre across the day. A varied plate most days gets you a meaningful amount without any single food needing to carry it. This week is about routine, everyday fibre intake as an ordinary nutrient target — if you''re currently dealing with an active bout of constipation, Week 72 ("Eating well when you''re constipated yourself") covers that specifically. Swapping some refined grain for a whole-grain version, keeping the skin on fruit and vegetables you''d normally eat whole, and including dal or legumes regularly are simple, practical ways to raise fibre. Building it up gradually over a week or two, alongside steady fluid intake, sits more comfortably than an abrupt change, which can bring on bloating or gas. This week focuses on everyday fibre sources and a gradual approach, and on when fibre guidance genuinely needs to be individualised for you.',
   'If you have a diagnosed digestive condition: such as IBS or inflammatory bowel disease, follow your own care team''s specific fibre guidance rather than this week''s general framing — fibre type and amount are often individualised for these conditions. If you''re recovering from gastroenteritis or a stomach bug: reintroduce fibre-rich food gradually as your digestion settles, rather than all at once. Persistent bloating, new or ongoing abdominal pain, or a marked change in your usual bowel pattern are worth mentioning to your own doctor rather than adjusting fibre intake on your own without knowing the cause. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s fibre, sodium or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on millet-forward Deccan and Karnataka cooking (ragi mudde, jowar roti, bajra khichdi, and similar). If a named dish isn''t familiar or easy to source where you are, a simple substitute works just as well — any whole-grain roti or khichdi from your own regional cooking, paired with dal and a vegetable, covers the same nutritional role described here.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Whole grains, dal and other legumes, vegetables, fruit, nuts and seeds all contribute fibre across the day — a varied plate most days gets you a meaningful amount without any single food needing to carry it. This week is about routine, everyday fibre intake, not managing an active bout of constipation (Week 72 covers that specifically).", "breakfast_a": "Ragi mudde (2 balls, about 100g cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with jowar toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bajra khichdi (1 cup) with a bhindi sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g)", "dinner_a": "Nachni roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with nachni roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Whole grains carry more fibre than their refined versions", "notes": "Swapping some white rice or maida-based food for brown rice, whole-wheat roti, bajra, jowar or ragi is a straightforward way to raise your day''s fibre without changing much else about the meal.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with whole-wheat toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with jowar roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted ajwain", "dinner_a": "Jowar roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with jowar roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Dal and legumes are a fibre source, not just protein", "notes": "Dal, rajma, chana and other legumes carry meaningful fibre as well as protein — worth thinking of them as a fibre source too, not just a protein one.", "breakfast_a": "Sprouted moong chilla (2 pieces, sprouts thoroughly cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Ragi mudde (2 balls, about 100g cooked) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with ragi mudde (2 balls, about 100g cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Raise fibre gradually, not all at once", "notes": "Adding several high-fibre foods together in one big jump can bring on bloating or gas — building fibre up gradually over a week or two, alongside steady fluid intake, sits more comfortably than an abrupt change.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion sandwich (2 eggs, whole-wheat bread, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bajra khichdi (1 cup) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with bajra khichdi (1 cup) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a fig", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-pea curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Fruit and vegetables, skin on where you''d normally eat it", "notes": "The skin of fruits and vegetables you''d normally eat whole — apple, guava, cucumber, potato — carries extra fibre that peeling removes; washing thoroughly and keeping the skin on, where that''s how you''d usually eat it, is a simple way to keep more of it.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach paratha (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable, skin left on where applicable", "lunch_b": "Grilled fish (100g fish) with jowar roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable, skin left on where applicable", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava, unpeeled and washed thoroughly, with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Nachni roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup, carrot skin left on)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer, carrot skin left on) with nachni roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When fibre guidance genuinely differs for you", "notes": "If you have a diagnosed digestive condition — IBS, inflammatory bowel disease, or a similar diagnosis — fibre type and amount often need individualising rather than following a general target; follow your own care team''s specific guidance instead of this week''s default framing.", "breakfast_a": "Jowar upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bajra roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with bajra roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted flaxseed (1 tsp, ground) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Jowar roti (2 pieces) with a lobia-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with jowar roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Which small swap this week — a whole grain, keeping a fruit''s skin on, a dal-forward meal — felt easiest to keep going with?", "breakfast_a": "Ragi mudde (2 balls, about 100g cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with jowar toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bajra khichdi (1 cup) with a bhindi sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g)", "dinner_a": "Nachni roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with nachni roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is fibre something you''ve thought about only when things feel backed up, rather than as part of your everyday plate — and would a small, steady change be easier than waiting for that moment?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — your own protein needs.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
