-- postpartum week 94: How much protein do you actually need right now (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   94,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much protein do you actually need right now',
   '"Protein needs vary with body size, activity, breastfeeding and health conditions. Include meaningful protein sources across the day; an extra serving may help, but it does not establish that your individual requirement is met."',
   'Protein needs vary with body size, activity level, whether you''re currently breastfeeding, and any health conditions you manage — there''s no single number that applies to everyone. Breastfeeding does raise protein needs somewhat above pre-pregnancy levels. Including meaningful protein sources across the day is a reasonable general step; an extra measured serving of dal, paneer, egg, meat or fish may help, but it doesn''t by itself establish that your individual requirement is met. Dal, rajma, chana, soy and paneer are all useful protein sources — dal, rajma, chana and soy are plant-based, while paneer, like curd and milk, is a dairy protein. Spread across breakfast, lunch, a snack and dinner, they add up meaningfully over the day. A cup of dal, a palm-sized piece of paneer, two eggs and a palm-sized piece of meat or fish carry different amounts of protein for a similar portion size, so varying your sources through the week is more dependable than assuming any one swap covers the same ground. This week focuses on including a measured protein source at more meals through the day, especially breakfast, and on when protein guidance genuinely needs to be individualised for you.',
   'If you have kidney disease or another condition where protein intake is prescribed and monitored: follow your own care team''s specific target rather than this week''s general framing. If you have gout: purine-rich animal proteins (organ meat, some fish and shellfish) may need moderating under your own doctor''s guidance. If you have a history of kidney stones: the right approach depends on your stone type — ask your own doctor before changing your protein intake. For some stone types, plant proteins like dal and legumes are a reasonable alternative to animal protein rather than something that automatically needs restricting. Persistent fatigue, unexplained muscle loss, or slow wound healing are worth mentioning to your own doctor rather than assuming more protein alone will resolve them. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s protein, sodium or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, and are useful protein sources. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Andhra and Telangana cooking (pesarattu, gongura, natukodi, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Your protein needs depend on your body size, activity level, whether you''re currently breastfeeding, and any health conditions you manage — there''s no single number that applies to everyone. Breastfeeding does raise protein needs somewhat; including a measured serving of dal, paneer, egg, meat or fish at more meals is a reasonable general step, but it doesn''t by itself establish that your own individual requirement is being met.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu (2 pieces, moong dal dosa) with ginger chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gongura pappu (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Natukodi (country-chicken curry, 100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted chana (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a paneer-and-vegetable side (50g paneer)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Vegetarian protein sources add up across the day", "notes": "Dal, rajma, chana, soy and paneer are all useful vegetarian protein sources — though paneer, like curd and milk, is a dairy protein rather than a plant one, while dal, rajma, chana and soy are plant-based. Spread across breakfast, lunch, a snack and dinner rather than concentrated in one meal, they add up meaningfully over the day, including on a fully vegetarian day.", "breakfast_a": "Besan-and-vegetable chilla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-curry-leaf bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gutti vankaya (stuffed brinjal curry, 3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana dal", "lunch_b": "Fish pulusu (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gutti vankaya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Soybean curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Different protein foods aren''t interchangeable in amount", "notes": "A cup of dal, a palm-sized piece of paneer, two eggs and a palm-sized piece of meat or fish all carry noticeably different amounts of protein for the same portion size — worth varying your sources across the week rather than assuming any one swap covers the same ground.", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu with onion topping (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tamarind chutney toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gongura pappu (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Andhra-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted almonds (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dates (3-4 pieces)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Protein alongside a good breakfast, not just at dinner", "notes": "Many days end up protein-light at breakfast and protein-heavy at dinner by habit rather than by plan — including a measured protein source at breakfast too, alongside your usual carbohydrate-forward option, spreads it more evenly across the day.", "breakfast_a": "Paneer paratha (2 pieces, 50g paneer filling) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-capsicum curry, Telangana-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted sunflower seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Gongura pappu (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-gongura curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A busy day is still a chance to include protein", "notes": "On a rushed day, a boiled egg, a handful of roasted chana, a glass of milk or a small bowl of curd are all quick, portable ways to keep protein in the picture without needing a full cooked meal.", "breakfast_a": "Boiled eggs (2) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Roasted chana (30g) with a small bowl of curd (150g), toast (2 slices) and a piece of fruit", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Royyala iguru (prawn curry, 100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted cashews (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a gongura-moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Gutti vankaya curry (100g paneer added) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When protein guidance genuinely differs for you", "notes": "If you have kidney disease or another condition where protein intake is prescribed and monitored, follow your own care team''s specific target rather than this week''s general framing — a higher protein intake isn''t automatically appropriate for every mother regardless of her medical history.", "breakfast_a": "Upma with sooji and vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mint toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Pesarattu (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Andhra-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted pistachios (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few figs", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a gongura-chana curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-gongura curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did you notice a meal this week where protein was easy to overlook — and did shifting it around make a difference to how the day felt?", "breakfast_a": "Pesarattu (2 pieces, moong dal dosa) with ginger chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Gongura pappu (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Natukodi (country-chicken curry, 100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted chana (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a paneer-and-vegetable side (50g paneer)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is your protein spread fairly evenly across the day, or does it cluster at one meal out of habit rather than by plan?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — calcium and your own bone health.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 95: Calcium and your own bone health, during and after breastfeeding (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   95,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Calcium and your own bone health, during and after breastfeeding',
   '"Some bone-density loss during breastfeeding is usually temporary, and bone density generally recovers after weaning — not a sign your calcium intake has failed you. Keeping calcium steady still supports that recovery."',
   'If you''re currently breastfeeding, some calcium is drawn from your bones to support milk production, largely regardless of how much calcium you eat. Some bone-density loss during breastfeeding is usually temporary, and bone density generally recovers after weaning and the return of your periods — this isn''t evidence that your intake has been inadequate. Persistent bone pain or a low-trauma fracture needs assessment rather than being assumed to be a normal breastfeeding change. Keeping calcium intake steady still supports the usual recovery, alongside vitamin D and weight-bearing movement. Curd, paneer, buttermilk, milk, ragi, sesame and calcium-fortified alternatives all contribute — calcium is something several parts of your day can carry, not something one drink alone is responsible for. Calcium and vitamin D work together but aren''t the same nutrient: ordinary milk or curd reliably supplies calcium but not reliably meaningful vitamin D — Week 89 covers vitamin D specifically. This week focuses on varied calcium sources through the day, and on when bone-health guidance genuinely needs individual attention.',
   'If you have a personal or strong family history of osteoporosis: or take long-term steroid medication, mention this to your own doctor — you may benefit from individual bone-density monitoring beyond this week''s general framing. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from calcium or iron supplements. A personal or family history of a fracture from a minor fall, or persistent unexplained bone pain, needs assessment by your own doctor rather than being assumed to be a normal breastfeeding change. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s calcium, sodium or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Kashmiri and Himachali cooking (dum aloo, chaman, madra, siddu, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re currently breastfeeding, some calcium is drawn from your bones to support milk production, largely regardless of how much calcium you eat. This bone-density loss is usually temporary and not evidence that your intake has been inadequate — bone density generally recovers after weaning and the return of your periods. Persistent bone pain or a fracture from a minor fall needs assessment rather than being assumed to be a normal breastfeeding change.", "breakfast_a": "Kashmiri kulcha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cheese toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kashmiri dum aloo (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chaman curry", "lunch_b": "Rogan josh, mild (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chaman curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted til (1 tsp)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chaman (Kashmiri paneer curry, 100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Calcium comes from more than just a glass of milk", "notes": "Curd, paneer, buttermilk, ragi, sesame (til) and calcium-fortified alternatives all contribute — worth thinking of calcium as something several parts of your day can carry, not something only one drink is responsible for.", "breakfast_a": "Siddu (steamed wheat bun, 2 pieces) with ghee and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-walnut bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Madra (chickpea-and-curd curry, 3/4 cup, made with 1/2 cup curd) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Himachali-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted til (1 tsp)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of dry ginger powder", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup), a cooked vegetable, and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Calcium and vitamin D work together, but aren''t the same nutrient", "notes": "Calcium is the building block; vitamin D helps your body actually absorb it. Ordinary milk or curd is a reliable calcium source but doesn''t reliably supply meaningful vitamin D on its own — Week 89 covers vitamin D food sources and supplement guidance specifically, worth revisiting alongside this week.", "breakfast_a": "Kashmiri vegetable uttapam-style paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml, check the label)", "lunch_a": "Nadru yakhni (lotus-stem curd curry, 3/4 cup, made with 1/2 cup curd) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Kashmiri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a fresh apricot", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of vitamin-D-fortified milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chaman-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you avoid dairy, this still applies to you", "notes": "Calcium-fortified plant milk, calcium-set tofu, ragi, sesame and green leafy vegetables can build toward your day''s calcium if you avoid dairy — check labels for fortified products, since ''fortified'' quantities vary by brand, and a fortified alternative matched on calcium isn''t automatically matched on protein too.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-stuffed kulcha (2 pieces) with a glass of calcium-fortified soy milk (200ml, check the label)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of calcium-fortified soy milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup) and a sesame-tossed beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Rogan josh, mild (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of calcium-fortified soy milk (200ml) with roasted til (1 tsp)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of calcium-fortified soy milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and sesame-tossed spinach", "dinner_b": "Calcium-set tofu curry (100g tofu) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "Calcium-set tofu, cubed (75g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Weight-bearing movement matters for bone health too", "notes": "Alongside calcium and vitamin D, regular weight-bearing movement — walking, light strength work, or whatever activity your own recovery allows — supports bone density; this is a nutrition-and-movement pairing worth knowing, not a suggestion to start anything beyond what your own body is currently ready for.", "breakfast_a": "Siddu (steamed wheat bun, 2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kashmiri dum aloo (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a rajma-tomato curry", "lunch_b": "Grilled fish, Himachali-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted pine nuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dried apricots (3-4 pieces)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chaman-and-carrot curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When bone health guidance genuinely differs for you", "notes": "If you have a personal or strong family history of osteoporosis, take long-term steroid medication, or have another condition affecting bone density, mention this to your own doctor — you may need individual monitoring or guidance beyond this week''s general framing.", "breakfast_a": "Madra (chickpea-and-curd curry, 3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-saffron toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Nadru yakhni (lotus-stem curd curry, 3/4 cup, made with 1/2 cup curd) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Kashmiri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a mandarin", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chaman-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did learning that some bone-density dip during breastfeeding is usually temporary change how you think about your own calcium intake?", "breakfast_a": "Kashmiri kulcha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cheese toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kashmiri dum aloo (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chaman curry", "lunch_b": "Rogan josh, mild (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chaman curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted til (1 tsp)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chaman (Kashmiri paneer curry, 100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Beyond food, is there a small, weight-bearing movement habit — walking, light strength work — that your own recovery currently allows and that you''d enjoy building in?',
