-- postpartum week 96: Getting enough iodine in your own diet (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   96,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Getting enough iodine in your own diet',
   '"Use iodised salt within your usual salt limit. It contributes iodine, but may not meet your full requirement — other foods, and where appropriate supplement guidance, also matter, especially while breastfeeding or if you avoid dairy, eggs and seafood."',
   'Iodine is essential for your own thyroid hormone production, and your needs rise notably while you''re breastfeeding. Using iodised salt as your everyday salt, within whatever total salt amount already fits your diet and any blood pressure guidance you follow, is a simple and useful step — the point is the TYPE of salt, not eating more of it. It contributes iodine, but salt use varies too much day to day to guarantee it meets your full requirement on its own. Milk, curd, eggs and seafood also contribute meaningfully; vegetables can carry some iodine too, but the amount varies too much with local soil content to depend on consistently. Iodine needs and how well they''re met vary between individuals — especially while breastfeeding, or if you avoid dairy, eggs and seafood — so where appropriate, supplement guidance from your own doctor matters too, rather than assuming salt alone establishes adequacy. This week focuses on iodised salt as a main everyday source, plus dairy, eggs and seafood as a meaningful addition, and on when iodine guidance genuinely needs individual attention.',
   'If you have a diagnosed thyroid condition, including an autoimmune thyroid condition: don''t add an iodine supplement beyond your usual diet without your own doctor''s guidance — excess iodine can also affect thyroid function in some people. If you take levothyroxine: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from calcium or iron supplements — this week''s iodine focus doesn''t change that timing. If you manage high blood pressure: keep total salt within the limits already advised for you — using iodised salt doesn''t change that limit, only the type of salt within it. Unexplained fatigue, weight change, neck swelling, or a family history of thyroid disease are worth raising with your own doctor. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s iodine, sodium or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, and fish and other seafood are useful iodine sources too. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Konkani and Mangalorean coastal cooking (neer dosa, sol kadhi, coconut-based curries, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Iodine is essential for your own thyroid hormone production, and your needs rise notably while you''re breastfeeding. Using iodised salt as your everyday salt, within whatever amount already fits your usual diet, is a simple and useful step — but it contributes iodine rather than guaranteeing your full requirement, and dairy, eggs, seafood and, where appropriate, supplement guidance also matter, especially if you avoid any of those foods.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa (3 pieces, cooked with iodised salt) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Coconut-based moong dal curry (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mangalorean fish curry (100g fish, cooked with iodised salt) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Iodised salt, not more salt", "notes": "The point isn''t to eat more salt overall — it''s making sure the salt you''re already using is iodised, and checking the packet says so. This doesn''t change whatever total salt guidance already applies to you, including if you''re managing blood pressure.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna (steamed rice cakes, 3 pieces, cooked with iodised salt) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coconut chutney toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, coconut-based (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a chikoo", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a curry-leaf tempering", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-coconut curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Dairy, eggs and seafood are also useful sources", "notes": "Milk, curd, eggs, and seafood (when you eat it) all contribute iodine alongside iodised salt — a varied plate that includes these regularly adds a genuine margin on top of the salt itself.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato-chutney toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Prawn gassi (coconut-based prawn curry, 100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a coconut-based spinach-and-dal curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-coconut-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Vegetable-based iodine sources are unreliable on their own", "notes": "Vegetables can carry some iodine, but the amount varies too much with local soil content to depend on for a consistent intake — worth thinking of vegetables as a bonus rather than your main iodine source, with iodised salt and dairy carrying the dependable share.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup, cooked with iodised salt) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coconut-based (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted sunflower seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-coconut curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Check where your salt actually comes from", "notes": "If a lot of your salt comes from eating out, packaged snacks or pickles rather than salt added at home, it may not be iodised — worth checking labels where you can. This isn''t a reason to add extra salt to compensate for uncertain iodine intake elsewhere; if you''re concerned your intake is low, dairy, eggs, seafood and your own doctor''s guidance are better places to look than more salt.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa (3 pieces, cooked with iodised salt) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mangalorean fish curry (100g fish, cooked with iodised salt) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square (jaggery-coconut, 1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When iodine guidance genuinely differs for you", "notes": "If you have a diagnosed thyroid condition — including an autoimmune thyroid condition — don''t add an iodine supplement beyond your usual diet without your own doctor''s guidance, since excess iodine can also affect thyroid function in some people. If you take levothyroxine, continue it exactly as prescribed, maintaining any required separation from calcium or iron supplements.", "breakfast_a": "Kori rotti (rice crisps, 2 pieces, cooked with iodised salt) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coconut-based (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a fig", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia-tomato curry (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt)", "dinner_b": "Sol kadhi (kokum-coconut curry, 1 bowl) with paneer curry (100g paneer) and roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did you check whether the salt you use most — at home, and wherever else you eat — is actually iodised?", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa (3 pieces, cooked with iodised salt) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Coconut-based moong dal curry (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mangalorean fish curry (100g fish, cooked with iodised salt) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup, cooked with iodised salt)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a nutrient this batch of weeks covered — fat, fluids, fibre, protein, calcium, or iodine — that you''d genuinely never thought about before as something specifically yours to look after?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with a new stretch of everyday themes — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 97: Vitamin B12, still worth double-checking this far in (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   97,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Vitamin B12, still worth double-checking this far in',
   '"B12 comes reliably only from animal foods or fortified/supplemented sources. If your own diet leans plant-based, or you take a medicine that can reduce absorption, this is worth another look now — not just something you checked once, early on."',
   'Vitamin B12 supports your nerves, your red blood cells and how you make energy from food. It''s reliably found only in animal foods — meat, fish, eggs and dairy — or in foods and supplements fortified with it; plant foods that aren''t fortified essentially don''t contain usable B12. Your liver can store a supply of B12, which is part of why a genuine shortfall can take a while to show up rather than appearing right away. That makes it worth checking in on again periodically, rather than assuming an early postpartum check settles the question for good — especially if your own diet has shifted since then, or you''ve started a medicine that can affect absorption. This week focuses on where your B12 is actually coming from, what fortification does and doesn''t guarantee, and situations — a plant-based diet, certain long-term medicines — where it''s worth a closer, individual look.',
   'If you follow a vegetarian diet that includes dairy and eggs: these contribute B12, but adequacy depends on the amounts you eat and how well you absorb it — regular consumption alone doesn''t guarantee enough. Fortified foods or a supplement may still be needed, especially with restricted intake or an absorption problem (NIH vitamin B12 guidance). If you follow a vegan diet, or largely avoid dairy and eggs: B12 depends entirely on fortified foods (check the label for the actual amount per serving) or a supplement — not on eating more plant food generally. Ask your own doctor what dose and schedule is right for you. If you take metformin long-term, or a long-term acid-reducing medicine such as a PPI: these can gradually reduce B12 absorption — mention it to your doctor so your levels can be checked periodically if relevant. Do not stop prescribed medication yourself. If you''ve had gastric or bowel surgery, or have a diagnosed absorption condition: your B12 needs and monitoring should follow your own care team''s specific guidance rather than this general week. Persistent fatigue, tingling or numbness in the hands or feet, low mood, or memory and concentration changes are worth a clinical assessment, with tests if indicated, rather than assuming B12 based on symptoms alone. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, and are also dependable B12 sources. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and B12 content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Bengali cooking (luchi, cholar dal, begun bhaja, doi maach, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "B12 supports your nerves, red blood cells and energy. It''s reliably found only in animal foods — meat, fish, eggs and dairy — or in foods and supplements fortified with it; plant foods that aren''t fortified essentially don''t contain usable B12. Your liver can store B12 for a while, which is part of why a shortfall can take time to show up rather than announcing itself right away.", "breakfast_a": "Luchi (2 pieces) with alur dom and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Cholar dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a begun bhaja", "lunch_b": "Rui fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a begun bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Dimer curry (egg curry, 2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If your own diet is vegetarian or vegan", "notes": "A vegetarian diet that includes dairy and eggs regularly can contribute meaningful B12, but adequacy still depends on how much you actually eat and how well you absorb it — it isn''t guaranteed just by including these foods. A vegan diet gives essentially none — B12 there depends entirely on fortified foods (checking the label for the amount) or a supplement, not on eating more plant food generally, however varied or nutritious it is otherwise.", "breakfast_a": "Cholar dal paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Alu posto (poppy seed potato curry, 1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana dal", "lunch_b": "Kosha mangsho, Bengali mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a chana dal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fortified foods aren''t automatically enough", "notes": "A fortified cereal or plant milk''s label amount only counts if you actually eat that product regularly and in a normal serving — an occasional bowl doesn''t reliably add up to a full day''s worth. If a fortified food is your main B12 source, check the label amount and how often you actually eat it, rather than assuming the word \"fortified\" alone covers it.", "breakfast_a": "Radhaballabhi (dal-stuffed puri, 2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Shukto, mixed vegetable curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cholar dal", "lunch_b": "Chingri malai curry, coconut prawn curry (100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cholar dal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a pear", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Symptoms alone don''t confirm it", "notes": "Fatigue, tingling or numbness in the hands or feet, low mood, and memory or concentration changes can all point toward low B12 — but they overlap heavily with plenty of other ordinary postpartum causes, from disrupted sleep to iron or thyroid changes. If any of these are persistent or worsening, that''s worth a clinical assessment, with tests if indicated, rather than assuming B12 based on symptoms alone.", "breakfast_a": "Chirer pulao, flattened rice pulao (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dhokar dalna, lentil-cake curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a chikoo", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Some medicines reduce absorption over time", "notes": "Long-term metformin use, and long-term use of acid-reducing medicines like PPIs, can both gradually lower how much B12 your body absorbs from food — this is a known effect, not a sign you''re doing anything wrong. If you''re on either of these long-term, it''s worth mentioning to your doctor so your B12 can be checked periodically if relevant, rather than assuming diet alone explains any low result.", "breakfast_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with cholar dal and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Alu-potol posto, potato and pointed gourd in poppy seed curry (1 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal", "lunch_b": "Doi maach, yoghurt fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a moong dal", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a fig", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "No single meal needs to \"cover\" your B12 for the week — what matters is a dependable source appearing regularly across your days. A freshly cooked plate and safely reheated leftovers count equally toward that; it''s the pattern that adds up, not any one perfectly composed meal.", "breakfast_a": "Muri makha, spiced puffed rice (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mint toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Cholar dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lau ghonto, bottle gourd curry", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Bengali-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lau ghonto, bottle gourd curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with roasted sunflower seeds (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a chikki square, jaggery-sesame (1 piece)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Lunch''s fish option is swapped for chicken today, since the week already includes three seafood occasions. Has your own diet — or a medicine you''re taking — changed since the last time you actually thought about your B12?", "breakfast_a": "Luchi (2 pieces) with alur dom and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Cholar dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a begun bhaja", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Bengali-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a begun bhaja", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Dimer curry (egg curry, 2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is there a nutrient conversation from earlier in this series that''s worth revisiting now, simply because time has passed and your own situation may have shifted since then?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another everyday nutrient worth a closer look — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;
